      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-4471",
        "knowledge_point_id": "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator",
        "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}}.$",
        "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{1-1}{1-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-\\sqrt{x})(x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1}}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
